# TLS in front of the app.
#
# The room is reached by name only, and that is not cosmetic: YouTube refuses
# to authorise label-owned videos when the embedding page's origin is a bare IP
# address, answering the player with error 150 and an "auth" code for
# essentially all commercial music. The same video, with the same player
# parameters, plays from a hostname. Serving the IP at all would only invite
# people onto an address where the music is broken, so it is not served.
#
# Cloudflare terminates TLS for visitors and connects back here over HTTPS in
# its "Full" mode, which encrypts that hop without verifying the certificate on
# it. The certificate here therefore comes from Caddy's own internal CA: there
# is no public ACME to run for a name whose port 80 belongs to Cloudflare, and
# nothing to renew from outside. No browser ever sees it; browsers only ever
# see Cloudflare's edge certificate. This is also what retired the previous
# Let's Encrypt certificate for the bare IP, which came from the "shortlived"
# profile, expired every six days, and made the renewal loop load-bearing.
#
# Nothing listens on port 80: Cloudflare speaks to this origin over 443, and no
# ACME challenge needs answering. Turning the Cloudflare proxy off would mean
# publishing 80 again so Caddy could pass an HTTP-01 challenge for a public
# certificate.
#
# There is deliberately no default_sni. It existed because TLS cannot carry an
# IP address in SNI, so a browser opening https://<ip> sent no server name and
# Caddy had nothing to match a certificate against. Both browsers and
# Cloudflare send a hostname, so a connection arriving with no SNI is one
# aimed at the bare IP, and failing its handshake is the intent.
#
# The admin API is left enabled so deploys can reload this file without
# dropping connections. It binds to localhost inside the container only, and no
# port is published for it.

https://{$SITE_HOST} {
	tls internal

	# Handles the Socket.IO websocket upgrade as well as normal requests.
	reverse_proxy plugdj:3000
}
